    "text" : "Entropy Change - System",
    "reading_order" : 27,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "heading",
    "heading_level" : 2,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000028",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**Spec point** — `spcpt_Bk5FqhdfBsqvxY6G`",
    "reading_order" : 28,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000029",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Entropy Change in the System",
    "reading_order" : 29,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "heading",
    "heading_level" : 2,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000030",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Entropy changes are an order of magnitude smaller than enthalpy changes, so entropy is measured in joules rather than kilojoules. The full unit for entropy is** J K**<sup>**-1 **</sup>**mol**<sup>**-1**</sup>",
    "reading_order" : 30,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000031",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The standard entropy change (Δ*S*<sup>Θ</sup><sub>*system*</sub>) for a given reaction can be calculated using the standard entropies (*S*<sup>*Θ*</sup><sup> </sup>) of the reactants and products",
    "reading_order" : 31,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000032",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The equation to calculate the standard entropy change of a system is:",
    "reading_order" : 32,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000033",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**Δ*****S***<sup>**Θ**</sup><sub>***system***</sub><sup>** **</sup>**= Σ*****S***<sup>**Θ**</sup><sub>***products***</sub>** - Σ*****S***<sup>**Θ**</sup><sub>***reactants***</sub>",
    "reading_order" : 33,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000034",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "(where Σ = sum of)",
    "reading_order" : 34,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000035",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "For example, the standard entropy change for the formation of ammonia (NH<sub>3</sub>) from nitrogen (N<sub>2</sub>) and hydrogen (H<sub>2</sub>) can be calculated using this equation",
    "reading_order" : 35,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000036",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**            N**<sub>**2**</sub>**(g) + 3H**<sub>**2**</sub>**(g) **$\\rightleftharpoons$** 2NH**<sub>**3**</sub>**(g)**",
    "reading_order" : 36,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000037",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**Δ*****S***<sup>**Θ**</sup><sub>***system***</sub>** = (2 x *****S***<sup>**Θ**</sup>**(NH**<sub>**3**</sub>**)) - (*****S***<sup>**Θ**</sup>**(N**<sub>**2**</sub>**) + 3 x *****S***<sup>**Θ**</sup>**(H**<sub>**2**</sub>**))**",
    "reading_order" : 37,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000038",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Notice that, unlike enthalpy of formation for elements, entropy for elements is **not** zero and you can find entropy values for elements and compounds in data books",
    "reading_order" : 38,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000039",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**Worked Example**",
    "reading_order" : 39,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
